.TH NTPDATE 8
.SH NAME
ntpdate \- set the system clock from NTP servers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ntpdate
.RB [ \-46bBdqsuv ]
.RB [ \-a " key" ]
.RB [ \-e " delay" ]
.RB [ \-k " file" ]
.RB [ \-o " version" ]
.RB [ \-p " samples" ]
.RB [ \-t " timeout" ]
.I server ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Query the named NTP servers and select a time sample.
Set the clock if the sample is suitable.
Without
.B \-b
or
.BR \-B ,
step the clock if the offset is 0.5 seconds or more, and slew it otherwise.
Changing the clock requires root privileges.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-4, \-6
Use IPv4 or IPv6.
.TP
.B \-b
Step the clock.
.TP
.B \-B
Slew the clock.
.TP
.B \-d
Print debugging output without changing the clock.
.TP
.B \-q
Query without changing the clock.
.TP
.B \-s
Send messages to syslog.
.TP
.B \-u
Use an unprivileged source port.
.TP
.B \-v
Log the version, then run as usual.
.TP
.BI \-a " key"
Authenticate replies with this key number.
.TP
.BI \-e " delay"
Set the authentication delay in seconds.
It must be less than 1.
.TP
.BI \-k " file"
Read authentication keys from this file.
The default is
.IR /etc/ntp.keys .
.TP
.BI \-o " version"
Use this NTP protocol version.
.TP
.BI \-p " samples"
Take this many samples from each server, from 1 to 8.
The default is 4.
.TP
.BI \-t " timeout"
Set the reply timeout in seconds.
It is at least 1, and the default is 2.
.SH EXAMPLES
Step the clock at boot:
.PP
.nf
ntpdate -b -u -p 1 -t 1 pool.ntp.org
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means a suitable server was found and the requested operation succeeded.
A query or debug run leaves the clock unchanged.
Status 2 means a usage error.
Any other nonzero status indicates failure.
